  "notes": "Conventions adopted, applied uniformly. (1) Attributes describe the standard, canonical form of each instrument (acoustic unless the name says otherwise: 'guitar' = classical/acoustic, 'electric guitar' = solid-body electric). (2) 'can sound three or more pitches at once' means chordal/polyphonic playing is standard technique for a single player. Bowed strings are FALSE under this rule: double stops are two pitches and triple stops are broken, not sustained. Bagpipes and hurdy-gurdy are TRUE because melody plus sounding drones is the normal state. Instruments whose idiom is monophonic melody (sitar, erhu, tenor steel pan, ondes Martenot, electric bass) are FALSE even where a chord is physically possible. (3) 'requires the player's breath' means lung air: bellows-driven instruments (accordion, harmonium, pipe organ) are FALSE. (4) 'is played with a bow' is read as a rosined friction exciter, so the hurdy-gurdy's rosined wheel counts as a bow. The nyckelharpa and hurdy-gurdy also count as keyboards (key/tangent box). (5) 'is sounded by plucking' is by mechanism, not by who does it: the harpsichord (quill plectra) is TRUE. Double bass is both bowed and plucked because pizzicato is a primary technique for it, unlike the rest of the violin family. (6) 'primary sounding element is metal' means the vibrating body, tube, bar, plate, tine or free reed is metal; strings alone do not count, and a metal shell under a skin head does not (so timpani, tabla and taiko are FALSE, glockenspiel, steel pan, tam-tam and accordion are TRUE). (7) 'originated outside Europe' is about where the instrument was invented/developed, not where it is played now, so American-invented electric and electronic instruments (electric guitar, synthesizer, Hammond organ, Rhodes, pedal steel, vibraphone, musical saw) are TRUE, while the theremin (St Petersburg) and ondes Martenot (Paris) are FALSE. Marimba is TRUE (Africa via Central America), banjo TRUE (African-American), cymbals TRUE (Anatolia/China), drum machine TRUE (Japan). (8) 'is portable by one person' = one adult can pick it up and carry it in normal use; the double bass counts (players transport their own), while a grand piano, concert harp, timpani, marimba, tam-tam, taiko, Hammond organ, Rhodes and ondes Martenot do not. (9) 'rests on the floor or a stand while being played' separates instruments supported by the ground/furniture (cello endpin, koto, tabla, snare stand, harmonium on the floor) from those held or strapped to the body (violin, accordion, crash cymbals, maracas). (10) Objects were chosen so that families which collapse to one row under these 14 attributes are represented once: one member of the oboe/clarinet/bassoon reed trio (clarinet), one brass instrument (trumpet), one viol (viola da gamba), one plucked-lute-per-region. The single remaining duplicate pair is concert flute / trumpet, which differ only by lip-reed excitation, a distinction no attribute here encodes; both were kept because omitting either would leave a conspicuous hole in a musical-instrument context.",
